WEBVTT

1
00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:15.800
Yeah.

2
00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:19.920
So thank you very much,
everyone, for coming.

3
00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:23.680
Yeah, that's the title of my presentation.

4
00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:27.520
I guess I'll just jump right into it.

5
00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:30.880
So just a preface,
but this is a working chapter.

6
00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:32.560
It's part of my upcoming thesis.

7
00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:37.000
So I do feel like some of my stuff
is sort of half-baked.

8
00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:40.720
So I welcome any suggestions
and reading recommendations

9
00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:44.880
that anyone feels like
is relevant to my project.

10
00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:48.880
And if anyone has read my abstract, 
I just want to say that like,

11
00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:52.720
some stuff has changed
since I submitted the original abstract.

12
00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:56.760
So I hope it's not too bewildering,
but I've just added some—

13
00:42:56.800 --> 00:43:01.680
like an additional concept
to the theoretical framework. So

14
00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:04.560
from the title,

15
00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:08.120
I want to start off
by introducing melancholy.

16
00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:11.120
So in more recent history,

17
00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:14.320
I think everyone might
or might not be familiar.

18
00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:18.560
Melancholy has sort of been construed
as an illness to be cured.

19
00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:23.320
Sigmund Freud specifically theorized it
as a kind of mourning

20
00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:27.400
that has gone wrong,
where the subject is unable to detach,

21
00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:32.360
so to speak, from the lost object,
which manifests as

22
00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:35.640
these symptoms right here,
like a loss of interest

23
00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:40.960
in the outside world and inhibition
of any kind of performance,

24
00:43:40.960 --> 00:43:44.360
and a self-hate and self-flagellation.

25
00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:49.000
And how is this— why is this important?

26
00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:52.000
It's because

27
00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:55.080
politically— sorry.

28
00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:58.680
Melancholy has sort of come
to characterize

29
00:43:58.680 --> 00:44:03.320
our historical moment 
after the collapse of the Soviet Union—the

30
00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:09.200
USSR—there seemed for a long time
a lack of a capitalist alternative, i.e.

31
00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:13.920
neoliberal— liberalism
seemed to have won the ideological battle.

32
00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:17.720
We see this from Francis Fukuyama’s
“end of history,”

33
00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:22.280
this quote from Furey
and this from Fredric Jameson right here,

34
00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:25.560
that encapsulate this sense of finality

35
00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:29.160
with respect to, like, our politics.

36
00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:34.160
And Wendy Brown more specifically targets

37
00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:38.040
the intellectual or the Western left.

38
00:44:38.040 --> 00:44:42.920
For Wendy Brown, they identify these

39
00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:48.240
finalized sort of narratives as having
a sort of dispiriting effect on the left

40
00:44:48.240 --> 00:44:53.480
and, by extension, the collective impetus
to think outside of capital.

41
00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:57.640
For Wendy Brown,
who was writing in 1999,

42
00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:02.080
the left she saw was paralyzed
by this sense of melancholy.

43
00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:06.000
They became more preoccupied
with mourning their loss

44
00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.440
and with regrouping
and investing in political change.

45
00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:16.040
On the contrary, more recently, other
theorists like Jonathan Flatley, pictured

46
00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:20.840
here, have begun to think melancholy
as something that can be more productive.

47
00:45:21.120 --> 00:45:26.520
Jonathan Flatley, for one, conceives
of it as melancholizing

48
00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:31.800
as a kind of method, and this falls
under his larger framework of affective

49
00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:37.560
mapping, which, in his words, names
a particular set of aesthetic strategies

50
00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:42.440
that allow one to perceive the historicity
of one's affective experience.

51
00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:48.040
His idea of mapping
takes some inspiration from

52
00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:52.640
Fredric

53
00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:56.600
Jameson, who talked about cognitive
mapping

54
00:45:56.640 --> 00:46:01.120
as a way of exercising
or like finding political agency.

55
00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:05.000
I thought the idea of cognitive mapping
was best summarized

56
00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:10.120
in this quote here, where he compares 
cognitive mapping to physical mapping.

57
00:46:10.280 --> 00:46:15.400
For Jameson, therefore, the inability
to situate oneself vis-à-vis

58
00:46:15.440 --> 00:46:19.240
society
and history is as debilitating to politics

59
00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:24.400
as not having a map for reference
is to navigating urban spaces.

60
00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:27.960
So going back to Flatley.

61
00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:32.760
I read Flatley’s reading of Jameson
and his idea of affective mapping

62
00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.080
as saying one's
writing of melancholy—because

63
00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:40.400
he was mainly working with literary texts
here—is a way of making sense

64
00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:44.880
of a certain experience
and then sharing that sense-making

65
00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:50.360
with the reader, and this framework
then guides the reader in the future.

66
00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:54.600
To put it simply,
it provides a sort of location

67
00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:57.800
of this melancholy affect in relation—

68
00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:01.920
and puts it in relation
to the historical moment and beyond.

69
00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:04.800
This brings me to

70
00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:08.600
the idea of melancholic agency, 
as Flatley himself

71
00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:12.920
says, with reference to Jameson's idea
of cognitive mapping:

72
00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:18.720
just as one needs a cognitive map
of city space in order to have

73
00:47:18.760 --> 00:47:23.800
a sense of agency in that space, one
requires a cognitive map of social space

74
00:47:23.800 --> 00:47:29.600
for a sense of agency in the world
more generally or politically.

75
00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:35.280
This dovetails
nicely with Brigid Bargetz’s

76
00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:39.400
borrowing the title of Donna
Haraway, “Staying with the Trouble,”

77
00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:43.800
suggests that we must stay
with the melancholy in order to remain

78
00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:48.040
attuned with the political struggles
of the past, and hence to the larger

79
00:47:48.040 --> 00:47:53.640
picture of the leftist project, which is,
you know, to forward social change.

80
00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:56.720
In other words,
to go back to Wendy's Brown,

81
00:47:56.760 --> 00:48:01.120
Wendy Brown's charge of left melancholy
as a thing of paralysis.

82
00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:04.240
Bargetz is saying perhaps some of this

83
00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:07.640
melancholy is productive, or in some ways

84
00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:11.240
necessary
for the future of anti-capitalism.

85
00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:16.400
So how does this relate to video games
and electronic literature?

86
00:48:16.640 --> 00:48:19.440
I'm kind of,
I suppose, seeking to build on

87
00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:23.200
Flatley's idea of affective mapping,
since his work mainly

88
00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:29.040
deals in modernist literature
and therefore more textual in the old

89
00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:31.560
sense of the medium.

90
00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:35.280
I ask, how do video games,
as an aesthetic form,

91
00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:38.760
channel these emancipatory desires?

92
00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:41.920
How do they render
the post-capitalist imaginary

93
00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:47.680
and therefore act as an affective map
for this resistive struggle

94
00:48:47.720 --> 00:48:51.280
alongside other art forms like literature?

95
00:48:53.440 --> 00:48:57.200
I think as all of us
at this electronic literature

96
00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:58.400
conference can agree,

97
00:48:58.400 --> 00:49:01.520
there are many similarities
between video games and literature,

98
00:49:01.520 --> 00:49:06.440
so I hope the connection
isn't too crazy to make.

99
00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:10.280
But in many ways,
I feel like video games as a new art form

100
00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:14.000
borrows from and adapts
from the literature of old.

101
00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:17.960
Firstly, video games are quite

102
00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:22.000
literally narrative worlds,

103
00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:25.960
and in some sense again
borrows from the old,

104
00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:29.600
but also uses a new set
of aesthetic strategies

105
00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:33.320
to build an aesthetic effect—

106
00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:36.560
affective map, so to speak,

107
00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:40.520
and also, and more obviously,
virtual space sort of lends itself

108
00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:44.160
to the idea of mapping,
and I kind of would like to explore that

109
00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:47.960
sort of connection,
like how does a physical

110
00:49:48.160 --> 00:49:53.160
or rather like more literal space function
as an affective map?

111
00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:56.560
Over here,
I thought Henry Jenkins’ “Narrative

112
00:49:56.560 --> 00:50:00.160
Architecture and Embedded
Narratives” would be instructive.

113
00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:04.960
But for the interest of time,
I won't go too into that.

114
00:50:04.960 --> 00:50:07.200
And onto Disco Elysium.

115
00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:12.000
It's one of my key texts
that I will be examining for my thesis,

116
00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:16.360
and to answer those earlier questions,
and to deepen this discussion.

117
00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:18.880
I will elaborate on it in a bit.

118
00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:21.800
But before that,
for those that aren't familiar

119
00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:25.960
with the game, it is a roleplaying,
point-and-click adventure game

120
00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:30.840
that follows the troubled
detective pictured here on the right,

121
00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:35.960
who, after some drug and alcohol binge,
has forgotten his identity.

122
00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:40.840
But he's been tasked with investigating
a murder that happened in the district

123
00:50:40.840 --> 00:50:46.200
of Martinaise, a fictional district 
which is a sort of backwater neighborhood

124
00:50:46.960 --> 00:50:49.520
that, in the not-so-distant past,

125
00:50:49.520 --> 00:50:54.480
had been embroiled in a revolutionary war
fought between the communists

126
00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:57.880
and an international neoliberal
governing body,

127
00:50:57.880 --> 00:51:01.480
which ultimately resulted
in the communists’ defeat.

128
00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:05.320
This murder—
You know, this sets the background

129
00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:08.840
of the murder, because the murder involves
the hanging of a mercenary,

130
00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:13.120
which was hired by a big shipping company
operating out of Martinaise

131
00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:17.120
by the local trade union
paying this mercenary.

132
00:51:17.120 --> 00:51:21.120
And through this investigation,
the player

133
00:51:21.160 --> 00:51:25.240
sort of pieces together
the story and and the narrative world,

134
00:51:25.240 --> 00:51:29.880
but also the protagonist's own identity,
because he's sort of in a fugue state.

135
00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:32.880
So the player gets to know

136
00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:36.240
a lot about the history,
but also the character.

137
00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:40.320
And these things
sort of intertwine at a certain point.

138
00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:44.560
And gameplay wise,

139
00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:49.160
Disco Elysium features very little combat
and is modeled after tabletop

140
00:51:49.200 --> 00:51:53.600
RPG games, where events are resolved
through skill checks, i.e.

141
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:55.640
a series of dice rolls.

142
00:51:55.640 --> 00:52:00.720
And you can see
in this sort of screenshot

143
00:52:00.760 --> 00:52:04.680
here, you have to roll a certain number
to pass the check,

144
00:52:04.680 --> 00:52:08.120
and therefore to proceed
like successfully or

145
00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:10.720
results in a failure.

146
00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:14.400
And dialog trees are,

147
00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:18.440
you know, explored
using a system of 24 skills

148
00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:21.920
representing the protagonist's
different aspects

149
00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:26.960
and personalities,
each of whom can speak directly

150
00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:31.640
to our detective here, 
aka our player— Sorry.

151
00:52:31.840 --> 00:52:37.720
And therefore influence their decision
making throughout the story.

152
00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:41.000
So what does Disco Elysium

153
00:52:41.600 --> 00:52:44.840
add specifically to this discussion,
and how does it exemplify

154
00:52:45.240 --> 00:52:49.840
the concept of affective
mapping and melancholic agency?

155
00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:55.360
Evidently, I feel like Disco
Elysium is quite politically charged

156
00:52:55.360 --> 00:53:00.880
and tells a rather melancholic
set of nested narratives.

157
00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:05.000
What, with the drunken detective
and failed revolution

158
00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:08.720
on the part of the communists, 
which mirrors our own.

159
00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:13.400
And this melancholy is reflected
in the landscape, in its bleak setting,

160
00:53:13.400 --> 00:53:17.400
and also the gameplay mechanics,
or rather lack thereof.

161
00:53:17.440 --> 00:53:20.480
On the latter point,
on gameplay mechanics, offers

162
00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:23.560
the most differentiation,
I think, to traditional literature, and

163
00:53:23.560 --> 00:53:28.160
so I'll try to spend some time
elaborating on it in the interest of time.

164
00:53:29.320 --> 00:53:32.680
Disco Elysium
is, I think, for the conventional gamer,

165
00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:35.960
a boring game because there's nothing
much really happens.

166
00:53:35.960 --> 00:53:39.240
There's no combat
and no mechanical difficulty.

167
00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:42.600
You don't have to defeat any bosses
by like pressing the right buttons

168
00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:46.400
at the right time, and the player
isn't really afforded much agency

169
00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:50.600
compared to your usual open world games,
like defeating of enemies,

170
00:53:50.600 --> 00:53:53.600
you can't really roam around a big open
world.

171
00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:56.560
There's no dramatic power scaling, etc.

172
00:53:56.560 --> 00:54:00.720
and I read this lack,
so to speak, as both reflective

173
00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:04.040
and evocative of that melancholic affect.

174
00:54:04.040 --> 00:54:10.120
The gameplay, in effect, attempts
to capture a sense of ennui and loss

175
00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:14.840
suffusing ordinary life,
while also telling a story

176
00:54:14.840 --> 00:54:20.680
that contextualizes these effects
in relation to revolutionary loss.

177
00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:26.720
To give a brief example, I think I'm
definitely running out of time, but

178
00:54:26.720 --> 00:54:31.560
the game sort of acclimatizes the player
to failure through the skill checks.

179
00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:33.760
It's very unlikely
that you pass every skill check

180
00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:36.160
and sometimes you find yourself
failing them.

181
00:54:36.160 --> 00:54:40.760
And as players, we've often been trained
to react negatively to failure.

182
00:54:40.800 --> 00:54:45.320
But in Disco Elysium, failures
sometimes give you sort of funny outcomes.

183
00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:47.360
For instance, in the opening of the game,

184
00:54:47.360 --> 00:54:52.520
there's a check where if the player fails,
you flip off an NPC character

185
00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:56.400
while running backwards, and then you
crash into a woman in a wheelchair.

186
00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:01.000
And it's really funny, as opposed
to just running away successfully.

187
00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:04.360
And the former, apart from being,
you know, kind of funny,

188
00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:08.400
also opens up a lot more dialog options
with the surrounding NPCs

189
00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:12.760
who never would have said certain things
if the player hadn't failed to check.

190
00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:16.680
And so this affective response
from the player

191
00:55:16.760 --> 00:55:22.360
initially might be disappointment
and in some sense like a sense of loss,

192
00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:26.240
but they are soon attuned
to the alternative possibilities

193
00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:29.040
and pathways
that are attached to this failure.

194
00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:33.760
And combined with the overarching sort
of political narrative of the game,

195
00:55:33.800 --> 00:55:38.640
one can sort of see the gameplay as a sort
of mapping of failure and melancholy

196
00:55:38.680 --> 00:55:43.680
to pathways of possibilities,
and in turn providing the player

197
00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:48.920
with an affective guide, so to speak,
for how their emotions and desire

198
00:55:48.960 --> 00:55:52.720
can be channeled,
reoriented, and remapped.

199
00:55:53.880 --> 00:55:57.360
And we're coming
to the end of the presentation, I swear.

200
00:55:57.360 --> 00:56:01.520
But here is where I have some unanswered
questions that perhaps— like

201
00:56:01.600 --> 00:56:05.440
if the audience has any responses,
feel free.

202
00:56:05.440 --> 00:56:10.800
But I think I'm having trouble making
sense of the ambivalence of melancholy,

203
00:56:10.800 --> 00:56:15.880
because I think it's clear that
melancholy is not inherently resistive.

204
00:56:15.880 --> 00:56:18.880
It's not inherently

205
00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:20.200
revolutionary.

206
00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:24.720
You can also be used for conservative
or reactionary purposes, as we've seen in

207
00:56:25.480 --> 00:56:30.560
the left melancholy and therefore like,
how do I make sense of this ambivalence?

208
00:56:30.560 --> 00:56:36.600
Is there a way to attune ourselves
to the more emancipatory strands of it?

209
00:56:36.600 --> 00:56:41.000
And if anyone is familiar with the game,
are there any other gameplay features

210
00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:44.400
that might be missing
or should pay more attention to?

211
00:56:44.560 --> 00:56:50.040
And with that, thank you so much
for paying attention and for being here.

212
00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:52.960
And I've come to
the end of my presentation.
